===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|graffito}}

===Etymology===
{{bor+|en|it|graffiti}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɡɹəˈfiː.ti/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ɡɹəˈfi.ti/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-graffiti.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːti|s=3}}
* {{hyph|en|graf|fi|ti}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|*}}

# {{senseid|en|drawings on a public surface}} {{lb|en|chiefly|uncountable}} [[drawing|Drawings]] or [[word]]s drawn on a [[surface]] in a public place, usually made without [[authorization]].
#: {{ux|en|The underpass is a popular place for '''graffiti''' artists.}}
#: {{ux|en|The city council spends thousands of pounds removing '''graffiti''' from public buildings.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2021 October 20|title=Network News: NR and NH tackle '''graffiti''' vandalism|journal=RAIL|issue=942|page=9|text=The removal of unsightly '''graffiti''' on the sides of railway bridges spanning major roads in the North West is to be handed over to National Highways, to speed up the job.}}
# {{lb|en|archaeology|countable}} Informal [[inscription]]s, figure drawings, etc., as opposed to official inscriptions.
#: {{syn|en|cave painting|epigraphy}}

====Usage notes====
* There is no universal singular form to denote a single piece of graffiti. In archaeology, and occasionally elsewhere, {{m|en|graffito}} is used, reflecting the Italian singular. There is some non-standard usage of {{m|en|graffitus}}, as though it were Latin (compare {{m|en|focus}}, plural {{m|en|foci}}); {{m|en|graffitum}}, also Latin sounding (though this would technically form the plural ''*graffita''; compare {{m|en|millennium}}, plural {{m|en|millennia}}); and ''graffiti'' itself, unmodified.

